{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-334021" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the set of functions ${\\,7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x\\in R,$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,+\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/state.json" "file_sha256" "48bc43d5e6530a4043e3df74cd58c559d2e3e3e7db65742ff7d3e5a0365109f0"} {"question" "q-334021" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n0\\cdot 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+\\,4\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,+\\,4\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{not all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{dependent}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/state.json" "file_sha256" "48bc43d5e6530a4043e3df74cd58c559d2e3e3e