2x)"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/state.json" "file_sha256" "48bc43d5e6530a4043e3df74cd58c559d2e3e3e7db65742ff7d3e5a0365109f0"} {"question" "q-79891" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/state.json" "file_sha256" "48bc43d5e6530a4043e3df74cd58c559d2e3e3e7db65742ff7d3e5a0365109f0"} {"question" "q-79893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ for ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/state.json" "file_sha256" "48bc43d5e6530a4043e3df74cd58c559d2e3e3e7db65742ff7d3e5a0365109f0"} {"question" "q-79893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as:\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =({x}^{2}){({x}^{3})}^{′}-({x}^{3}){({x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =({x}^{2})(3{x}^{2})-({x}^{3})(2x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{4}-2{x}^{4} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/state.json" "file_sha256" "48bc43d5e6530a4043e3df74cd58c559d2e3e3e7db65742ff7d3e5a0365109f0"} {"question" "q-79893" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f31a0fbdf99cfda2ea86e857f382e4d5ec78a2be790cf94c511fd56e9942be3e"} {"question" "q-79893" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/state.json" "file_sha256" "48bc43d5e6530a4043e3df74cd58c559d2e3e3e7db65742ff7d3e5a0365109f0"} {"question" "q-79893" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-x"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2{x}^{3}"] ["math" "3{x}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/state.json" "file_sha256" "48bc43d5e6530a4043e3df74cd58c559d2e3e3e7db65742ff7d3e5a0365109f0"} {"question" "q-79893" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/state.json" "file_sha256" "48bc43d5e6530a4043e3df74cd58c559d2e3e3e7db65742ff7d3e5a0365109f0"} {"question" "q-333517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x+1},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x},\\,x\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ above. Given that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/state.json" "file_sha256" "48bc43d5e6530a4043e3df74cd58c559d2e3e3e7db65742ff7d3e5a0365109f0"} {"question" "q-333517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ {({e}^{2x+1})}^{′} & {({e}^{2x})}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+1} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x+1} & 2{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{2x+1}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x+1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{4x+1}-2{e}^{4x+1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/state.json" "file_sha256" "48bc43d5e6530a4043e3df74cd58c559d2e3e3e7db65742ff7d3e5a0365109f0"} {"question" "q-333517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f31a0fbdf99cfda2ea86e857f382e4d5ec78a2be790cf94c511fd56e9942be3e"} {"question" "q-333517" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "f