{y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are defined on some interval $I.$\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of these functions is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W\\ne 0$ for at least one $x\\in I,$ then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If $W=0$ and it’s known that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ ({e}^{x})^{′} & ({e}^{2x})^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & {e}^{2x} \\\\ {e}^{x} & 2{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x}-{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/state.json" "file_sha256" "48bc43d5e6530a4043e3df74cd58c559d2e3e3e7db65742ff7d3e5a0365109f0"} {"question" "q-334121" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f31a0fbdf99cfda2ea86e857f382e4d5ec78a2be790cf94c511fd56e9942be3e"} {"question" "q-334121" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062380/state.json" "file_sha256" "48bc43d5e6530a4043e3df74cd58c559d2e3e3e